{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>He shall not pour oil upon it</b> — so that her offering should not be adorned, for oil is called light, and she acted in darkness. <b>And he shall not put frankincense upon it</b> — because the Matriarchs are called לבונה, as it is stated (Song of Songs 4:6), \"To the hill of frankincense,\" and she has departed from their ways (Tanhuma Naso 3). <b>For it is a meal-offering of jealousies</b> — this flour stirs up in her two jealousies: the jealousy of the Place and the jealousy of the husband (Sifrei Naso 50). This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Numbers 5:15), from the words of our Sages.\n\nAnd according to the opinion of all the commentators, <b>for it is a meal-offering of jealousies</b> — to say that because this meal-offering is a meal-offering of punishment, it is diminished by being barley without oil or frankincense, since the meal-offering whose mention comes \"for a pleasing odor to YHWH\" consists of fine flour of wheat with oil and frankincense.\n\nIn my opinion, the reason for <b>for it is a meal-offering of jealousies</b> refers back to the beginning of the verse: it says that the man shall bring the offering on behalf of his wife — that is, in her place — for it is a meal-offering of jealousies, to remind of her iniquity, and it is not fitting that she should bring it from her own property; rather, he shall bring a meal-offering to HaShem, that He may exercise His jealousy and avenge His vengeance upon her.\n\nAnd the reason for the barley is that it signifies the storm of YHWH's wrath — \"חמה\" (wrath) going forth, \"סער\" (storming) breaking out, about to alight upon the head of the wicked woman. It is like the dream of the barley mentioned in the case of Gideon (Judges 7:13), which they interpreted as a storm and great confusion. And likewise the earthen vessel is a sign that she shall be broken like a potter's vessel, and likewise the dust, for she is dust and to dust she shall return.\n\nAnd the reference of <b>it is</b> is to the flour, and it has a secret meaning. For below (Numbers 5:18), when it is in the woman's hand, it says \"היא\" (she/it), and now He mentioned the masculine form first and said that it (הוא) brings iniquity to remembrance:"], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 5, :notes {0 "The final sentence is textually difficult: Ramban contrasts the masculine pronoun הוא in our verse (v. 15) with the feminine pronoun היא below (v. 18), then says 'He mentioned the masculine first,' but the logic of the contrast and the phrase 'ואמר כי היא מזכרת עון' ('and said that it [feminine] brings iniquity to remembrance') is not fully coherent in the extant text; rendered hyper-literally as it stands."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:03:04.501181781Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}